   166	**Repository scope (B and C only):** `parley-deck/` lives in a git repo. It may sit at the root of a dedicated coordination repo, or as a top-level directory inside the project's main code repo. The implementation phase typically operates on a _code repo_, which may equal the parley-deck repo (monorepo case) or be a different repo. When they differ, the **design PR/MR lives in the parley-deck repo** and the **implementation PR/MR lives in the code repo**, cross-referencing each other by URL.
   167	
   168	## 4. Protocol — phases of an idea
   169	
   170	This section describes the **conceptual** flow and the **artifacts** produced. The transport-specific _mechanics_ (commits vs PRs, comments vs files, merging vs status flags) are in §11.
   171	
   172	### 4.0 — Track selection (conditional rigor)
   173	
   174	Not every change needs the full lifecycle. Each idea runs on one of three **tracks**, set as
   175	`track: fast | standard | deliberation` in `00-prompt.md` (default `standard`). The track is a
   176	**mechanical routing decision** from the classifier below — it scales ceremony to risk. Round
   177	content (analysis, review, refutation) is always model-driven; only the routing is objective.
   178	
   179	**Classifier — check the `deliberation` triggers first; first match wins:**
   180	
   181	| → `deliberation` if ANY                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              | → `fast` if ALL                                                                                              | → `standard` (default)                                              |
   182	| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |
   183	| protocol change (§7); security / auth / secrets / payments / privacy / production infra; data migration / irreversible / destructive op; `strict_gate: true`; `auto_implement`; pipeline or action block (§12); public-API or persisted-schema break; > ~15 files / ~1000 LOC | fully reversible; ≤ ~3–5 files / ~300 LOC; no security or data surface; mechanically verifiable (lint / type / test) | everything that is neither forced to `deliberation` nor fully `fast` |
   184	
   185	**Classifier ordering is normative and fail-safe:** evaluate all `deliberation` triggers
   186	first; if none fire, evaluate the `fast` conditions; otherwise `standard`. On any doubt or
   187	boundary case — the 6–14-file band, the `~300`–`~1000` LOC gap, or an unconfirmed-but-plausible
   188	security / privacy / production / data-migration / pipeline / API-break / schema-break
   189	trigger — **fail closed to the stricter track**: `standard` over `fast`, and `deliberation`
   190	over `standard`, until the risk is disproven.
   191	
   192	**Per-track behavior** (the invariants below hold on every track):
   193	
   194	| Aspect                        | `fast`                                     | `standard` (default)                        | `deliberation`                          |
   195	| ----------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------- |
   196	| §9.0 readiness ping           | skipped                                     | full                                        | full                                    |
   197	| Cross-review rounds (Phase 2) | skipped                                     | capped at 2, then escalate/upgrade          | unbounded                               |
   198	| Consensus + FINAL (Phase 3–4) | collapsed: one `FINAL.md` with embedded signoffs | separate, drafted simultaneously        | separate                                |
   199	| Reviewers (Phase 6)           | 1 (model-diverse)                           | 2                                           | all non-implementers                    |
   200	| Review consensus (Phase 7)    | the one reviewer's ✅ = consensus            | reviewers who reviewed sign off             | all participants sign off               |
   201	| Fix-up (Phase 8)              | cap 1 cycle; fix-only verification ok       | cap 2 cycles; fix-only verify for narrow fixes | unbounded; `strict_gate` available   |
   202	| Timeout per agent             | ~5 min                                      | ~15 min                                     | ~30 min                                 |
   203	| Auto-advance                  | full (pause only for the one signoff)       | auto-advance; human gate at FINAL→implementation | human gate at each transition      |
   204	
   205	**This table is the single authoritative per-track gate. It OVERRIDES the full-lifecycle
   206	defaults stated in the rest of §4 and in §5 (quorum), §9.0 (readiness), and §11 (transport).**
   207	Where a later phase says "every participant," "all reviewers," or "consensus is reached when
   208	every active participant signs off," read it through this table for `fast`/`standard`;
   209	`deliberation` uses the rule exactly as written elsewhere.
   210	
   211	**Invariants on every track (never dropped for speed):** at least one independent
   212	non-facilitator artifact (non-solo, §1); refutation-default review — the reviewer *count*
   213	shrinks by track, the refutation discipline never does; round-1 independence (Phase 1);
   214	append-only ✅/🟡/❌ signoffs; files-canonical audit trail; the §14 human brake; English-only;
   215	no-secrets. (Rules tagged `LE-N` below are the loop-engineering rules; the tag is only a
   216	reference id — the rule text is what binds.)
   217	
   218	**Binding, challenge & mid-idea upgrade.** The track is binding once Phase 0 closes, but any
   219	participant may **force-upgrade** to a stricter track — an `inbox/` note before round-1 closes,
   220	or a reviewer filing a MAJOR/CRITICAL finding that cites a trigger. Down-tiering below the
   221	classifier floor requires a recorded user OK. If implementation later reveals a higher-risk
   222	surface (e.g. it now touches auth), any participant force-upgrades and the idea **re-runs the
   223	current phase** under the stricter track's rules. Upgrading from `fast` also reinstates any
   224	phase `fast` skipped (cross-review, a separate consensus/FINAL step) for the remainder of the
   225	idea. With only two participants, `standard`'s "2 reviewers" degrades to 1 (the trigger
   226	accounts for roster size, not just risk).
